1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,750
There are about 16,000 ETFs in the world.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,920
Together, they hold around 24 trillion dollars.

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:09,590
But almost all of them fit

4
00:00:09,610 --> 00:00:10,880
into one of twelve boxes.

5
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:13,040
Learn the boxes,

6
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:15,160
and you'll know exactly what you're buying.

7
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:16,470
In 11 minutes.

8
00:00:17,770 --> 00:00:19,620
No hype, and no stock picks.

9
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:21,530
ETFs can lose value.

10
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:24,020
This is education, not financial advice.

11
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:27,100
First, what is an ETF?

12
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,030
It's a basket of investments.

13
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:29,860
One share gives you

14
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:32,090
a small slice of everything inside.

15
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:36,280
ETF stands for exchange-traded fund.

16
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:37,250
It trades on a stock

17
00:00:37,270 --> 00:00:39,160
exchange like a normal share,

18
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:40,030
all day long.

19
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:43,030
Instead of buying hundreds

20
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:43,950
of shares one by one,

21
00:00:43,970 --> 00:00:45,890
you buy a single ETF,

22
00:00:45,910 --> 00:00:47,710
and pay one small yearly fee.

23
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,800
In Europe, most ETFs follow

24
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:53,390
the EU's fund rules, called UCITS.

25
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:54,250
That's why their names

26
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:56,030
often end with those letters.

27
00:00:57,330 --> 00:00:59,730
UCITS funds keep your investments separate

28
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:01,310
from the company that runs them.

29
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:03,210
If that company goes bust,

30
00:01:03,230 --> 00:01:05,310
the investments still belong to the fund.

31
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,280
American ETFs like VOO usually don't

32
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,390
publish the EU's key information document.

33
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:13,650
So most European brokers

34
00:01:13,670 --> 00:01:15,140
won't sell them to you.

35
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:18,100
Let's open the first box.

36
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:20,140
Box 1.

37
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:21,500
Index ETFs.

38
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:23,970
The biggest box.

39
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:25,430
An index ETF simply

40
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:27,340
copies a list of companies,

41
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,900
like the S&P 500 or the MSCI World.

42
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,960
The MSCI World holds around 1,300 companies,

43
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:38,020
from 23 developed countries.

44
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:41,080
But more than two-thirds of it

45
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:42,140
is American companies.

46
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:44,160
The rest is spread across Japan,

47
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:45,200
Europe and others.

48
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,400
Bigger companies get a bigger weight.

49
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:49,730
When a company grows,

50
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:50,300
its share

51
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:52,620
of the index grows with it, automatically.

52
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:55,100
Nobody picks the stocks.

53
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:56,940
A computer follows the list,

54
00:01:56,960 --> 00:01:58,960
which is why the fees can be tiny.

55
00:02:00,270 --> 00:02:02,990
Some S&P 500 ETFs cost as

56
00:02:03,010 --> 00:02:06,330
little as 0.03% a year.

57
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:10,320
A typical bank fund often charges around 1.8%.

58
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:14,070
Invest €200 a month for 30

59
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:15,770
years at 7% a year,

60
00:02:15,790 --> 00:02:16,700
and that fee gap

61
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:19,170
can cost you more than €60,000.

62
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:22,950
Some index ETFs buy the shares directly.

63
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:24,960
That's called physical.

64
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:26,740
Others get the same return through

65
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:28,170
a contract with a bank.

66
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:29,690
That's called synthetic.

67
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,640
The first ETF launched in America in 1993.

68
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:36,230
Europe got its first

69
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:38,190
ones in 2000, in Frankfurt.

70
00:02:38,210 --> 00:02:40,230
Today, it's a giant industry.

71
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:42,310
Box 2.

72
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:44,120
Country and region ETFs.

73
00:02:45,430 --> 00:02:46,590
Some ETFs zoom

74
00:02:46,610 --> 00:02:48,310
in on one part of the world.

75
00:02:48,330 --> 00:02:50,400
Europe, Japan, emerging markets,

76
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:52,060
or even a single country.

77
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:56,860
Emerging markets, like China, India, Taiwan or Brazil,

78
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:58,200
can offer more growth,

79
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,350
but also more risk and bigger swings.

80
00:03:01,630 --> 00:03:03,160
And when you buy another country,

81
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:05,070
you also buy its currency.

82
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:07,520
A Japan ETF moves with the yen.

83
00:03:08,830 --> 00:03:11,400
Some investors combine a world ETF

84
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:13,580
with an emerging markets ETF.

85
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:16,940
Others pick one all-world ETF that holds both.

86
00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:19,560
Many people mostly buy

87
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:21,380
companies from their own country.

88
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:23,310
That's called home bias.

89
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:25,280
But even big European countries are only

90
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:27,760
a few percent of the world's stock market.

91
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:32,020
The catch with a single country is concentration.

92
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:35,330
Japan's stock market peaked in 1989,

93
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:37,660
and took 34 years to get back there.

94
00:03:38,970 --> 00:03:39,780
Box 3.

95
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:41,050
Sector ETFs.

96
00:03:42,330 --> 00:03:45,060
Sector ETFs hold just one industry.

97
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:48,080
Technology, healthcare, banks, energy, and so on.

98
00:03:49,370 --> 00:03:50,110
They can soar

99
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:51,910
when their industry is in fashion,

100
00:03:51,930 --> 00:03:53,290
and crash when it isn't.

101
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:57,140
Energy stocks fell hard in 2020,

102
00:03:57,160 --> 00:04:00,400
then became the best performing sector of 2022.

103
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:04,040
And if you already own a world ETF,

104
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:05,730
a tech ETF on top means

105
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:08,070
buying the same big companies twice.

106
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:12,370
A world ETF already holds every sector.

107
00:04:12,390 --> 00:04:13,910
A sector ETF is a bet

108
00:04:13,930 --> 00:04:15,340
on just one of them.

109
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:17,350
Box 4.

110
00:04:17,370 --> 00:04:18,770
Thematic ETFs.

111
00:04:20,070 --> 00:04:22,370
Thematic ETFs follow a story.

112
00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:26,370
Artificial intelligence, robots, clean energy, cybersecurity.

113
00:04:27,670 --> 00:04:28,620
They often launch

114
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:30,190
when the story is already hot,

115
00:04:30,210 --> 00:04:32,270
and the stocks are already expensive.

116
00:04:33,570 --> 00:04:35,840
Morningstar found that over 15 years,

117
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:39,330
only about 9% of thematic funds both survived

118
00:04:39,350 --> 00:04:40,710
and beat global stocks.

119
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:42,800
Many closed,

120
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:45,390
or lagged behind a simple world ETF,

121
00:04:45,410 --> 00:04:47,460
partly because of higher fees.

122
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:50,030
A good story isn't always a good investment.

123
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:52,100
Box 5.

124
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:53,440
Factor ETFs.

125
00:04:54,730 --> 00:04:57,340
Factor ETFs, also called smart beta,

126
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:58,940
tilt toward one trait.

127
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:02,290
Cheap stocks, small companies, momentum, or quality.

128
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:05,200
There's also equal weight.

129
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:07,520
Every company gets the same slice,

130
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:09,530
instead of letting the giants dominate.

131
00:05:10,830 --> 00:05:13,480
Academic research found that some of these traits

132
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:15,790
paid a little extra over very long periods.

133
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:18,760
But they can lag for years.

134
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:20,660
Value stocks trailed growth stocks

135
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:22,290
for most of the 2010s.

136
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,120
A factor ETF only works if

137
00:05:25,140 --> 00:05:26,300
you have the patience

138
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:28,070
to hold it through the bad years.

139
00:05:29,370 --> 00:05:30,030
Box 6.

140
00:05:30,050 --> 00:05:31,520
Dividend ETFs.

141
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:35,170
Dividend ETFs pick companies that pay out

142
00:05:35,190 --> 00:05:37,100
a lot of cash to their shareholders.

143
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,260
But a dividend isn't free money.

144
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:41,240
When it's paid out,

145
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:42,470
the share price drops

146
00:05:42,490 --> 00:05:43,900
by about the same amount.

147
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:46,160
A very high yield

148
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:47,830
can even be a warning sign.

149
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:49,340
Sometimes the price has fallen

150
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:51,270
because the business is in trouble.

151
00:05:52,570 --> 00:05:55,620
In Europe, many ETFs come in two versions.

152
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:58,070
Distributing pays the dividends to you.

153
00:05:58,090 --> 00:06:00,710
Accumulating reinvests them automatically.

154
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,550
Which one suits you depends

155
00:06:03,570 --> 00:06:05,110
on how your country taxes them,

156
00:06:05,130 --> 00:06:06,750
so check your local rules.

157
00:06:08,070 --> 00:06:08,890
Box 7.

158
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:10,000
Bond ETFs.

159
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:12,800
Bond ETFs hold loans,

160
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,910
to governments or to companies.

161
00:06:14,930 --> 00:06:17,140
You earn interest instead of profits.

162
00:06:18,430 --> 00:06:20,690
Government bonds from strong countries

163
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:22,010
are usually the safest.

164
00:06:22,030 --> 00:06:23,890
Company bonds pay more interest,

165
00:06:23,910 --> 00:06:25,490
because the risk is higher.

166
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:28,730
They usually move less than stocks,

167
00:06:28,750 --> 00:06:30,820
which is why many people mix the two.

168
00:06:32,130 --> 00:06:33,590
When interest rates go up,

169
00:06:33,610 --> 00:06:34,950
older bonds lose value,

170
00:06:34,970 --> 00:06:36,530
because new bonds pay more.

171
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:39,010
But not always.

172
00:06:39,030 --> 00:06:41,850
In 2022, interest rates jumped,

173
00:06:41,870 --> 00:06:44,470
and global bonds lost about 16%,

174
00:06:44,490 --> 00:06:45,840
their worst year on record.

175
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:49,000
Short-term bonds move less.

176
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:50,670
Long-term bonds move a lot more

177
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:52,320
when interest rates change.

178
00:06:53,630 --> 00:06:56,410
Bonds in another currency add currency risk.

179
00:06:56,430 --> 00:06:58,350
That's why many bond ETFs come

180
00:06:58,370 --> 00:07:00,130
in a version hedged to euros.

181
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:02,160
Box 8.

182
00:07:02,180 --> 00:07:03,750
Money market ETFs.

183
00:07:05,030 --> 00:07:07,780
Money market ETFs follow the interest rate

184
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,630
that banks pay each other overnight.

185
00:07:09,650 --> 00:07:11,580
In euros, it's called €STR.

186
00:07:12,870 --> 00:07:15,800
Their value climbs slowly, almost every day.

187
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:17,090
It's the closest thing

188
00:07:17,110 --> 00:07:19,360
to a savings account in ETF form.

189
00:07:20,670 --> 00:07:22,230
But it's not a bank account.

190
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:24,000
There's no deposit guarantee.

191
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:27,640
When rates are low, they earn almost nothing.

192
00:07:27,660 --> 00:07:29,110
When rates were negative,

193
00:07:29,130 --> 00:07:30,200
they even lost a little.

194
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:32,180
When rates rise, they earn more.

195
00:07:33,470 --> 00:07:34,170
Box 9.

196
00:07:34,190 --> 00:07:35,140
Commodities.

197
00:07:36,430 --> 00:07:37,950
Gold is the classic.

198
00:07:37,970 --> 00:07:38,200
But in Europe,

199
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:40,260
a regular fund isn't allowed

200
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:41,930
to hold just one commodity.

201
00:07:43,230 --> 00:07:45,230
So gold trades as an ETC,

202
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:47,400
an exchange-traded commodity.

203
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:49,000
Often, each share is backed

204
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:50,690
by real gold in a vault.

205
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,150
Gold has long dry spells too.

206
00:07:54,170 --> 00:07:56,420
In dollars, it peaked in 2011,

207
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:58,510
and only got back there in 2020.

208
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:03,070
Other commodities, like oil, often use futures contracts.

209
00:08:03,090 --> 00:08:05,060
Their returns can drift away from

210
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:06,860
the price you see on the news.

211
00:08:08,170 --> 00:08:11,280
And commodities pay no interest and no dividends.

212
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:13,240
You only gain if the price goes up.

213
00:08:14,530 --> 00:08:15,100
Box 10.

214
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:16,720
Leveraged and inverse.

215
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:19,750
Leveraged products promise two

216
00:08:19,770 --> 00:08:20,610
or three times

217
00:08:20,630 --> 00:08:22,110
the daily move of an index.

218
00:08:22,130 --> 00:08:23,230
Inverse ones go up

219
00:08:23,250 --> 00:08:24,740
when the market goes down.

220
00:08:26,030 --> 00:08:27,380
The key word is daily.

221
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:29,180
They reset every single day.

222
00:08:30,470 --> 00:08:32,410
If an index rises 10%,

223
00:08:32,430 --> 00:08:35,310
then falls 10%, it's down 1%.

224
00:08:35,330 --> 00:08:38,460
A two times leveraged product is down 4%.

225
00:08:39,770 --> 00:08:42,380
This loss is often called volatility decay.

226
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:43,940
The more the market zigzags,

227
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:45,080
the bigger it gets.

228
00:08:46,370 --> 00:08:47,470
In a choppy market,

229
00:08:47,490 --> 00:08:48,890
they can lose money even

230
00:08:48,910 --> 00:08:50,480
if the index ends flat.

231
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:53,300
They're built for short-term traders, not for holding.

232
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:55,490
Box 11.

233
00:08:55,510 --> 00:08:56,750
Active ETFs.

234
00:08:58,030 --> 00:08:59,330
Active ETFs have

235
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:01,040
a manager who picks the stocks,

236
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:02,960
inside an ETF wrapper.

237
00:09:04,270 --> 00:09:05,710
They usually cost more,

238
00:09:05,730 --> 00:09:07,730
and the manager has to beat the market

239
00:09:07,750 --> 00:09:08,700
after those fees.

240
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,740
According to S&P's long-running research,

241
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:14,390
most active funds lag their

242
00:09:14,410 --> 00:09:15,850
index over the long run.

243
00:09:17,170 --> 00:09:18,870
Some managers do beat it.

244
00:09:18,890 --> 00:09:20,140
The hard part is knowing

245
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:21,480
which ones, in advance.

246
00:09:22,770 --> 00:09:23,660
Box 12.

247
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:24,930
Crypto ETPs.

248
00:09:26,230 --> 00:09:27,350
The last box isn't

249
00:09:27,370 --> 00:09:29,050
technically an ETF at all.

250
00:09:30,330 --> 00:09:31,690
In Europe, a regular

251
00:09:31,710 --> 00:09:33,910
ETF can't hold bitcoin directly.

252
00:09:33,930 --> 00:09:36,350
So crypto products are ETPs,

253
00:09:36,370 --> 00:09:39,110
debt-like notes, often backed by real coins.

254
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:42,490
America approved its first spot

255
00:09:42,510 --> 00:09:45,170
bitcoin ETFs in January 2024.

256
00:09:45,190 --> 00:09:47,670
In Europe, they're still ETPs.

257
00:09:48,970 --> 00:09:49,920
You get the same

258
00:09:49,940 --> 00:09:51,730
wild price swings as crypto,

259
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:53,180
plus the risk of the company

260
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:54,410
that issues the note.

261
00:09:55,700 --> 00:09:57,070
If you want the full story,

262
00:09:57,090 --> 00:09:59,570
our last video explains every crypto,

263
00:09:59,590 --> 00:10:00,660
box by box.

264
00:10:01,970 --> 00:10:04,680
Before you buy any ETF, read its name.

265
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:06,280
It tells you the provider,

266
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:07,060
the index, the EU rules,

267
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:09,120
the fund currency,

268
00:10:09,140 --> 00:10:10,820
and what happens to the dividends.

269
00:10:12,130 --> 00:10:13,570
Then check three things.

270
00:10:13,590 --> 00:10:14,880
The yearly fee, called TER.

271
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:16,960
The size of the fund.

272
00:10:16,980 --> 00:10:19,080
And how closely it follows its index.

273
00:10:20,370 --> 00:10:22,100
Those are the twelve boxes.

274
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:23,680
Almost every ETF you'll see

275
00:10:23,700 --> 00:10:24,990
fits into one of them.

276
00:10:26,300 --> 00:10:28,550
Remember, ETFs can lose value,

277
00:10:28,570 --> 00:10:30,780
and nothing here is a recommendation.

278
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,870
This is not financial advice.

279
00:10:34,170 --> 00:10:35,710
Twelve boxes, eleven minutes.

280
00:10:35,730 --> 00:10:37,510
Now, when you see a new ETF,

281
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:39,290
you'll know which box it's in,

282
00:10:39,310 --> 00:10:40,250
and what to ask.

283
00:10:41,570 --> 00:10:43,260
If this helped, subscribe.

284
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:44,550
